1:1-1 (* EXPECT-ERROR: no implicit conversions: F64 + I64 *)
2:1-14 (* "There are no implicit conversions, including widenings", and there\n   was one, through the one name every loop has.  FOR bound its variable\n   afresh with NO type in the self-hosted checker.  So every use of a\n   loop variable was an operand of unknown type, and unknown never\n   diagnoses.  This program compiled, in the released 0.13.0 too, and\n   printed 6.50: an integer added to a real with nothing written to say\n   so.  `m9c --review` showed it on 2026-10-02.  Its page for Stats\n   listed a dozen sites "passed over, a type unknown" that had only `i`\n   in common.  The Pascal oracle typed the variable as an I64 whatever\n   its declaration, so the two checkers had disagreed for as long as\n   both existed and no probe had asked.  The loop variable is a variable\n   now: declared, of a type its bounds can count in, and of that type\n   inside the loop. *)
comments=2
